{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "penicillin_g",
      "title": "Penicillin G (benzylpenicillin)",
      "category": "Penicillins",
      "subtitle": "Narrow-spectrum penicillin | IV, IM",
      "summary": "Drug of choice for syphilis, susceptible streptococci, meningococcus and many anaerobes. Short half-life, so it needs frequent dosing or continuous infusion.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Typical IV dose: 2 to 4 million units every 4 hours (12 to 24 million units a day).\n• Meningitis: 24 million units a day IV, divided every 4 hours.\n• Streptococcal endocarditis: 12 to 18 million units a day IV, divided or as a continuous infusion; the regimen depends on the organism's MIC.\n• Early syphilis: benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM as a single dose; late latent syphilis: weekly for 3 doses.\n• Benzathine and procaine preparations are for IM use only and must never be given IV."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• CrCl 10 to 50 mL/min: about 75% of the usual dose.\n• CrCl below 10 mL/min: 20 to 50% of the usual dose.\n• High doses in renal failure can cause seizures (neurotoxicity)."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against Streptococcus pyogenes, susceptible S. pneumoniae, viridans streptococci, susceptible Neisseria meningitidis, Treponema pallidum, Clostridium species, Actinomyces and Listeria.\n• Not reliable for most S. aureus (penicillinase), Gram-negative bacilli, or enterococci, where ampicillin is the better choice."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Safe in pregnancy; the drug of choice for syphilis (desensitise if allergic) and for group B streptococcal prophylaxis in labour.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Penicillin G potassium contains about 1.7 mEq of potassium per million units, so watch potassium in renal failure.\n• Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction can follow the first dose in syphilis.\n• Check the allergy history: many labelled penicillin allergies are not true allergy.\n• Prolonged high-dose courses can cause neutropenia and haemolytic anaemia."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "amoxicillin",
      "title": "Amoxicillin",
      "category": "Penicillins",
      "subtitle": "Aminopenicillin | Oral (IV in some countries)",
      "summary": "First-line oral beta-lactam for respiratory, ear and dental infections and for Helicobacter pylori combinations. Inactivated by beta-lactamases.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Oral: 500 mg every 8 hours, or 875 mg every 12 hours.\n• High dose: 1 g every 8 hours (up to 3 g a day) for community-acquired pneumonia and penicillin-nonsusceptible pneumococci.\n• Endocarditis prophylaxis before dental procedures in the highest-risk patients: 2 g orally as a single dose 30 to 60 minutes before.\n• IV amoxicillin is available in some countries, for example 1 g every 8 hours, and up to 2 g every 4 hours for meningitis or endocarditis.\n• H. pylori: used within combination regimens, so follow the regimen's own dosing."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• CrCl 10 to 30 mL/min: 250 to 500 mg every 12 hours.\n• CrCl below 10 mL/min: 250 to 500 mg every 24 hours.\n• Haemodialysis: 250 to 500 mg every 24 hours, with a dose after dialysis.\n• Avoid the 875 mg tablet when CrCl is below 30 mL/min."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against streptococci, Enterococcus faecalis (ampicillin-susceptible), Listeria, and non-beta-lactamase-producing H. influenzae and E. coli.\n• Common uses: community-acquired pneumonia, otitis media, sinusitis, pharyngitis, dental infection, H. pylori combinations.\n• Not active against beta-lactamase producers (many S. aureus, H. influenzae, E. coli), MRSA or Pseudomonas."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Considered safe in pregnancy.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding; watch the infant for rash, diarrhoea and thrush."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Can be taken with or without food.\n• A non-allergic rash is common in infectious mononucleosis and with allopurinol.\n• High doses can cause crystalluria, so keep the patient hydrated.\n• May raise INR with warfarin, and probenecid raises amoxicillin levels; reduces methotrexate clearance.\n• Diarrhoea and C. difficile infection are possible."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "amoxicillin_clavulanate",
      "title": "Amoxicillin-clavulanate",
      "category": "Penicillins",
      "subtitle": "Beta-lactam with beta-lactamase inhibitor | Oral, IV (not US)",
      "summary": "Extends amoxicillin to beta-lactamase producers and anaerobes. Used for bites, sinusitis, otitis media, aspiration and mixed infections.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Oral: 500/125 mg every 8 hours, or 875/125 mg every 12 hours.\n• Extended-release 2000/125 mg every 12 hours (where available) for resistant pneumococci.\n• IV (not available in the US): 1.2 g (1 g/200 mg) every 8 hours, up to every 6 hours in severe infection.\n• Take at the start of a meal to improve absorption and reduce diarrhoea."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• CrCl 10 to 30 mL/min: 500/125 mg every 12 hours; do not use the 875 mg tablet.\n• CrCl below 10 mL/min: 500/125 mg every 24 hours.\n• Haemodialysis: 500/125 mg every 24 hours, with a dose after dialysis.\n• Do not use the 2000 mg extended-release tablet when CrCl is below 30 mL/min."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Covers beta-lactamase-producing H. influenzae, Moraxella, many E. coli and Klebsiella, methicillin-susceptible S. aureus and most oral anaerobes.\n• Common uses: animal and human bites, sinusitis, otitis media, aspiration pneumonia, mild diabetic foot infection, skin infection, and oral step-down for intra-abdominal infection.\n• Not active against Pseudomonas, MRSA or ESBL producers."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Generally considered safe in pregnancy.\n• Avoid in preterm prelabour rupture of membranes because of a possible link with necrotising enterocolitis in the newborn.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Tablets with different amoxicillin:clavulanate ratios are not interchangeable; do not substitute two 250/125 tablets for one 500/125.\n• Diarrhoea is common (the clavulanate component).\n• Cholestatic hepatitis can start days to weeks after stopping, more often in older men and with courses longer than 14 days; check liver tests in long courses.\n• C. difficile infection and hypersensitivity reactions are possible."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "ampicillin_sulbactam",
      "title": "Ampicillin and ampicillin-sulbactam",
      "category": "Penicillins",
      "subtitle": "Aminopenicillin (with beta-lactamase inhibitor) | IV",
      "summary": "Ampicillin is the drug of choice for Listeria and ampicillin-susceptible enterococci. Adding sulbactam covers beta-lactamase producers and gives sulbactam's own activity against Acinetobacter.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Ampicillin: 1 to 2 g IV every 4 to 6 hours.\n• Meningitis and Listeria: ampicillin 2 g IV every 4 hours.\n• Enterococcal endocarditis: ampicillin 2 g IV every 4 hours, combined with ceftriaxone or gentamicin.\n• Group B streptococcal prophylaxis in labour: 2 g IV, then 1 g every 4 hours until delivery.\n• Ampicillin-sulbactam: 3 g (2 g/1 g) IV every 6 hours.\n• Carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter: high-dose sulbactam, about 9 g of the sulbactam component a day in divided doses, often as extended infusions. See the IDSA AMR tab."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• Ampicillin: CrCl above 50 mL/min every 6 hours; 10 to 50 every 6 to 12 hours; below 10 every 12 to 24 hours.\n• Ampicillin-sulbactam: CrCl 30 or above, 3 g every 6 to 8 hours; 15 to 29, 3 g every 12 hours; 5 to 14, 3 g every 24 hours."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Ampicillin: Listeria, Enterococcus faecalis, group B streptococci, susceptible Salmonella and some other Gram-negatives.\n• Ampicillin-sulbactam: skin and soft tissue infection, aspiration pneumonia, community intra-abdominal infection, diabetic foot infection, and Acinetobacter baumannii.\n• E. coli resistance to ampicillin-sulbactam is common, so check local susceptibility. Not active against Pseudomonas or ESBL producers."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Considered safe in pregnancy.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Rash is common in infectious mononucleosis.\n• High doses in renal failure can cause seizures.\n• Contains sodium, so watch fluid balance in heart or renal failure.\n• Diarrhoea, C. difficile infection and phlebitis at the infusion site are possible."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition. Acinetobacter dosing: IDSA AMR guidance."
    },
    {
      "id": "antistaph_penicillins",
      "title": "Flucloxacillin, nafcillin and oxacillin",
      "category": "Penicillins",
      "subtitle": "Anti-staphylococcal penicillins | IV, oral",
      "summary": "Drugs of choice for methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA). Flucloxacillin is used in the UK, Europe and Australasia; nafcillin and oxacillin in the US.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Flucloxacillin IV: 1 to 2 g every 6 hours; up to 2 g every 4 hours (12 g a day) for endocarditis, osteomyelitis and severe infection.\n• Flucloxacillin oral: 500 mg to 1 g every 6 hours, taken on an empty stomach (1 hour before or 2 hours after food).\n• Nafcillin or oxacillin IV: 2 g every 4 hours (up to 12 g a day) for MSSA bacteraemia, endocarditis and bone infection.\n• Dicloxacillin oral (US): 250 to 500 mg every 6 hours for mild skin infection.\n• Oral step-down is not an equivalent alternative to IV therapy for S. aureus bacteraemia, because of poor oral bioavailability."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• Flucloxacillin: no change is needed for mild to moderate impairment; in severe renal failure (CrCl below 10 mL/min), follow local guidance to reduce the dose or lengthen the interval.\n• Nafcillin and oxacillin are mainly cleared by the liver, so no renal adjustment is usually needed.\n• Nafcillin: reduce the dose in combined severe renal and hepatic failure."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against methicillin-susceptible S. aureus and streptococci.\n• Not active against MRSA, enterococci or Gram-negative bacilli.\n• For MSSA bacteraemia, anti-staphylococcal penicillins or cefazolin are superior to vancomycin."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Considered safe in pregnancy.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Flucloxacillin: cholestatic hepatitis can appear up to 2 months after stopping, with higher risk above age 50 and with courses over 14 days; monitor liver tests.\n• Flucloxacillin with paracetamol can cause high anion gap metabolic acidosis (pyroglutamic acidosis), especially in women, malnourished patients and those with renal impairment.\n• Nafcillin: causes hypokalaemia and phlebitis; extravasation can cause tissue necrosis, so a central line is preferred for long courses; it reduces warfarin effect and lowers levels of some drugs through enzyme induction.\n• Oxacillin: can cause interstitial nephritis and raised transaminases.\n• Monitor full blood count and liver and renal function on prolonged therapy."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "piperacillin_tazobactam",
      "title": "Piperacillin-tazobactam",
      "category": "Penicillins",
      "subtitle": "Antipseudomonal penicillin with beta-lactamase inhibitor | IV",
      "summary": "Broad-spectrum cover for Gram-positives, Gram-negatives including Pseudomonas, and anaerobes. Workhorse for hospital-acquired and intra-abdominal infection, but not recommended for ESBL bloodstream infection.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Standard: 4.5 g (4 g/0.5 g) IV every 6 hours; 3.375 g every 6 hours for lower-severity infection.\n• Nosocomial pneumonia and Pseudomonas: 4.5 g every 6 hours.\n• Extended infusion (commonly used): 4.5 g over 4 hours every 8 hours, after an initial 30-minute loading dose.\n• Doses are expressed as total piperacillin plus tazobactam."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• CrCl above 40 mL/min: no adjustment.\n• CrCl 20 to 40: 2.25 g every 6 hours (3.375 g every 6 hours for nosocomial pneumonia).\n• CrCl below 20: 2.25 g every 8 hours (2.25 g every 6 hours for nosocomial pneumonia).\n• Haemodialysis: 2.25 g every 12 hours (every 8 hours for nosocomial pneumonia), plus 0.75 g after each dialysis session.\n• Local extended-infusion protocols may use different renal doses, so follow your hospital's guideline."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Covers streptococci, methicillin-susceptible S. aureus, Enterococcus faecalis, many Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas aeruginosa and anaerobes.\n• Common uses: hospital-acquired pneumonia, intra-abdominal infection, febrile neutropenia, diabetic foot infection, and sepsis of unknown source.\n• Not active against MRSA or most ESBL producers.\n• IDSA guidance recommends carbapenems rather than piperacillin-tazobactam for ESBL-E infections outside the urinary tract, based on the MERINO trial."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Considered acceptable in pregnancy when needed.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Combination with vancomycin is associated with a higher rate of acute kidney injury than vancomycin with cefepime or a carbapenem; monitor creatinine.\n• Contains sodium (about 2.35 mEq per gram of piperacillin), so watch fluid balance in heart failure and renal failure.\n• Hypokalaemia, cytopenias with prolonged courses, and raised liver tests can occur.\n• Piperacillin can reduce methotrexate clearance.\n• Do not mix with aminoglycosides in the same bag or line, because they inactivate each other.\n• Penicillin allergy history matters; cross-reactivity with cephalosporins is low."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition. ESBL guidance: IDSA AMR guidance."
    },
    {
      "id": "cefazolin",
      "title": "Cefazolin",
      "category": "Cephalosporins",
      "subtitle": "First-generation cephalosporin | IV, IM",
      "summary": "First choice for MSSA infections and for most surgical prophylaxis. A unique side chain makes cross-reactivity with penicillins unlikely, so it is usable in most patients labelled penicillin-allergic.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Typical: 1 to 2 g IV every 8 hours.\n• MSSA bacteraemia and endocarditis: 2 g IV every 8 hours.\n• Surgical prophylaxis: 2 g IV within 60 minutes before incision (3 g if the patient weighs 120 kg or more); repeat every 4 hours during long operations.\n• Cellulitis and bone or joint infection: 1 to 2 g every 8 hours, with the dose chosen by site and severity."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• CrCl 55 mL/min or above: usual dose.\n• CrCl 35 to 54: usual dose, with at least 8 hours between doses.\n• CrCl 11 to 34: half the usual dose every 12 hours.\n• CrCl 10 or below: half the usual dose every 18 to 24 hours.\n• Haemodialysis: dose after each session, for example 2 g after dialysis, or 2 g/2 g/3 g three times a week."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against methicillin-susceptible S. aureus, streptococci, and some Enterobacterales (E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus).\n• Not active against MRSA, enterococci, Listeria, Pseudomonas or anaerobes such as Bacteroides.\n• Does not penetrate the central nervous system, so it should not be used for meningitis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Considered safe in pregnancy.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Penicillin allergy: cefazolin shares no side chain with penicillins or other cephalosporins, so it can be given to most patients with a non-severe penicillin allergy.\n• Rash, diarrhoea and C. difficile infection are possible.\n• Can cause false-positive urine glucose tests with copper-reduction methods, and a positive Coombs test.\n• Prolonged courses need blood count and liver test monitoring."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "ceftriaxone",
      "title": "Ceftriaxone",
      "category": "Cephalosporins",
      "subtitle": "Third-generation cephalosporin | IV, IM",
      "summary": "Once-daily cephalosporin for pneumonia, meningitis, gonorrhoea, complicated urinary infection and many community infections. Excreted by both bile and kidney.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Standard: 1 to 2 g IV or IM every 24 hours.\n• Bacterial meningitis: 2 g IV every 12 hours.\n• Community-acquired pneumonia: 1 to 2 g every 24 hours, with a macrolide or doxycycline when atypical cover is needed.\n• Uncomplicated gonorrhoea: 500 mg IM as a single dose (1 g if the patient weighs 150 kg or more), per CDC guidance.\n• Endocarditis (streptococcal): 2 g every 24 hours; enterococcal endocarditis (with ampicillin): 2 g every 12 hours.\n• Lyme neuroborreliosis and disseminated Lyme disease: 2 g every 24 hours.\n• IM injections are given with 1% lidocaine to reduce pain."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• No adjustment for renal impairment alone.\n• In combined severe renal and hepatic impairment, do not exceed 2 g a day.\n• Haemodialysis: no supplemental dose is needed."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against streptococci, methicillin-susceptible S. aureus, Neisseria (including gonorrhoea and meningococcus), H. influenzae, and many Enterobacterales.\n• Not active against enterococci, Listeria, MRSA, anaerobes (moderate), or Pseudomonas.\n• Do not rely on it for serious infection with Enterobacter, Citrobacter or Serratia, which can develop AmpC resistance during therapy; and it is not suitable for ESBL producers."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Considered safe in pregnancy.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding; watch the infant for diarrhoea and thrush."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Never mix or give ceftriaxone with calcium-containing IV solutions in neonates, because of fatal precipitation; avoid in neonates with hyperbilirubinaemia.\n• In adults, do not give calcium-containing solutions through the same line at the same time.\n• Biliary sludge and pseudolithiasis can occur with high doses, and are more common in children.\n• Can cause haemolytic anaemia (rare), diarrhoea and C. difficile infection.\n• May increase the effect of warfarin; check INR."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition. Gonorrhoea: CDC STI guidance."
    },
    {
      "id": "cefalexin",
      "title": "Cefalexin (cephalexin)",
      "category": "Cephalosporins",
      "subtitle": "First-generation cephalosporin | Oral",
      "summary": "Oral workhorse for skin and soft tissue infection, uncomplicated urinary infection and streptococcal pharyngitis, and a common step-down after IV cefazolin.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Usual: 500 mg orally every 6 hours; 250 mg to 1 g every 6 hours depending on severity.\n• Twice-daily regimen for uncomplicated skin infection, pharyngitis or cystitis: 500 mg every 12 hours.\n• Maximum 4 g a day.\n• Can be taken with or without food."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• CrCl 30 mL/min or above: no adjustment is usually needed.\n• CrCl 15 to 29: 250 mg every 8 to 12 hours.\n• CrCl 5 to 14: 250 mg every 24 hours.\n• CrCl below 5: 250 mg every 48 to 60 hours.\n• Haemodialysis: dose after dialysis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against methicillin-susceptible S. aureus, streptococci, and some E. coli, Klebsiella and Proteus.\n• Common uses: cellulitis, impetigo, skin abscess after drainage (when cover is needed), streptococcal pharyngitis, uncomplicated UTI, and step-down after IV therapy.\n• Not active against MRSA, enterococci, Pseudomonas, anaerobes, or ESBL producers.\n• Poor choice for pyelonephritis in areas with high E. coli resistance."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Considered safe in pregnancy.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Raises metformin levels by reducing renal tubular secretion, so monitor for lactic acidosis in renal impairment.\n• Diarrhoea and C. difficile infection are possible.\n• Cross-reactivity with penicillins is low for a first-generation agent, but cefalexin shares a side chain with ampicillin and amoxicillin, so avoid it after a severe reaction to those drugs.\n• Can cause false-positive urine glucose tests with copper-reduction methods."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "cefuroxime",
      "title": "Cefuroxime",
      "category": "Cephalosporins",
      "subtitle": "Second-generation cephalosporin | IV, oral (axetil)",
      "summary": "Respiratory and skin cover with activity against H. influenzae, plus a first-line oral option for early Lyme disease. Do not use for meningitis.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• IV: 750 mg to 1.5 g every 8 hours.\n• Oral (cefuroxime axetil): 250 to 500 mg every 12 hours, taken with food to improve absorption.\n• Early Lyme disease: 500 mg orally every 12 hours for 14 days (14 to 21 days for erythema migrans per guidance).\n• Do not use for bacterial meningitis, because treatment failure and neurological sequelae have been reported."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• IV: CrCl above 20 mL/min no change; 10 to 20: 750 mg every 12 hours; below 10: 750 mg every 24 hours.\n• Oral: CrCl 10 to 29: usual dose every 24 hours; below 10: usual dose every 48 hours.\n• Haemodialysis: dose after dialysis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against streptococci, methicillin-susceptible S. aureus, H. influenzae (including beta-lactamase producers), Moraxella and some Enterobacterales.\n• Common uses: community-acquired pneumonia (with a macrolide when atypicals are suspected), sinusitis, otitis media, skin infection, and early Lyme disease.\n• Not active against MRSA, enterococci, Pseudomonas or anaerobes such as Bacteroides."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Considered safe in pregnancy.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Tablets are poorly absorbed on an empty stomach, so take them with food; do not crush them because of the bitter taste.\n• Not interchangeable with cefuroxime axetil suspension on a mg-for-mg basis, since the formulations differ.\n• Diarrhoea and C. difficile infection are possible.\n• H2 blockers and antacids reduce the absorption of cefuroxime axetil; space doses apart."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "ceftazidime",
      "title": "Ceftazidime",
      "category": "Cephalosporins",
      "subtitle": "Third-generation antipseudomonal cephalosporin | IV, IM",
      "summary": "Antipseudomonal cephalosporin with strong Gram-negative activity but weak Gram-positive activity. Mainstay for melioidosis and Pseudomonas infection when cefepime or other options are not suitable.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Typical: 2 g IV every 8 hours for Pseudomonas, febrile neutropenia, hospital-acquired infection and meningitis.\n• Melioidosis (intensive phase): 2 g IV every 6 to 8 hours for at least 10 to 14 days.\n• Lower-severity infection: 1 g IV or IM every 8 to 12 hours.\n• Can be given as an extended or continuous infusion; follow local protocol."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• Label bands (for a usual 1 g every 8 hours dose): CrCl 31 to 50: 1 g every 12 hours; 16 to 30: 1 g every 24 hours; 6 to 15: 500 mg every 24 hours; 5 or below: 500 mg every 48 hours.\n• When the usual dose is 2 g every 8 hours, use your hospital's renal dosing table, because the bands above are too low for severe infection.\n• Haemodialysis: give a dose after each session."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against many Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, H. influenzae, Neisseria, and Burkholderia pseudomallei.\n• Weak against Gram-positive organisms: not reliable for staphylococci or pneumococci.\n• No useful anaerobic activity, and not active against enterococci, MRSA or ESBL producers.\n• Resistance can emerge during therapy in AmpC producers."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Considered acceptable in pregnancy when needed.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Neurotoxicity (confusion, myoclonus, seizures) when the dose is not reduced for renal impairment.\n• Positive Coombs test is common; haemolytic anaemia is rare.\n• Diarrhoea and C. difficile infection are possible.\n• Reconstituted solutions release gas, so follow preparation instructions for the product.\n• Do not mix with aminoglycosides in the same line."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "cefepime",
      "title": "Cefepime",
      "category": "Cephalosporins",
      "subtitle": "Fourth-generation cephalosporin | IV, IM",
      "summary": "Broad cover for Gram-positives and Gram-negatives including Pseudomonas. First-line for febrile neutropenia and nosocomial pneumonia, and used for AmpC-producing Enterobacterales when the cefepime MIC is low. Neurotoxic in renal impairment.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Febrile neutropenia, nosocomial pneumonia and Pseudomonas infection: 2 g IV every 8 hours.\n• Moderate infection (for example pneumonia, UTI, skin infection): 1 to 2 g IV every 12 hours.\n• Meningitis (off-label but widely used): 2 g IV every 8 hours.\n• Extended infusions are used in some hospitals; follow local protocol."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
