          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against MRSA, coagulase-negative staphylococci, Enterococcus faecalis (ampicillin-resistant strains), streptococci, Clostridioides difficile (oral) and Corynebacterium.\n• Common uses: MRSA bacteraemia, endocarditis, pneumonia (with linezolid as an alternative), bone and joint infection, meningitis (with a cephalosporin), and device infection.\n• Inferior to anti-staphylococcal beta-lactams for MSSA, so de-escalate when susceptibility is known.\n• Not active against Gram-negatives; VRE and VISA or VRSA are resistant."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Used in pregnancy when needed, with levels guiding the dose because clearance rises.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Nephrotoxicity: risk rises with high troughs, long courses, critical illness and other nephrotoxins; vancomycin plus piperacillin-tazobactam carries a higher risk of acute kidney injury than alternatives.\n• Monitor creatinine at least two to three times a week, and daily in unstable patients.\n• Vancomycin infusion reaction (flushing from histamine release, not true allergy): slow the infusion and give an antihistamine.\n• Ototoxicity is uncommon, but more likely with high levels and aminoglycosides.\n• Neutropenia on prolonged courses, so check the blood count weekly.\n• Extravasation can cause tissue injury."
