          "text": "• QT prolongation and torsades de pointes: avoid or monitor with other QT-prolonging drugs (for example amiodarone, fluoroquinolones, ondansetron, haloperidol), and in patients with long QT, hypokalaemia or hypomagnesaemia.\n• Fewer cytochrome P450 interactions than erythromycin or clarithromycin, but it can increase digoxin levels (P-glycoprotein) and the effect of warfarin; check INR.\n• Antacids containing aluminium or magnesium reduce peak levels; take azithromycin at least 2 hours apart from them.\n• Hearing loss with prolonged high-dose courses, cholestatic hepatitis, and diarrhoea or C. difficile infection.\n• Long-term use for prevention requires a baseline ECG and screening for nontuberculous mycobacteria."
