      "title": "Rifampicin (rifampin)",
      "category": "Rifamycins",
      "subtitle": "Rifamycin | Oral, IV",
      "summary": "Bactericidal against mycobacteria and highly active against staphylococci in biofilm. Never used alone for treatment because resistance emerges rapidly. Notorious for powerful enzyme induction and many drug interactions.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose",
          "text": "• Tuberculosis: 10 mg/kg (usually 600 mg) orally or IV once a day, as part of a multidrug regimen.\n• Prosthetic joint and device infection with staphylococci: 300 to 450 mg orally every 12 hours, combined with another active drug, and started only after the wound is debrided and bacteraemia has cleared.\n• Meningococcal prophylaxis for close contacts: 600 mg every 12 hours for 4 doses (2 days).\n• H. influenzae type b prophylaxis: adults 600 mg once a day for 4 days; children 20 mg/kg (maximum 600 mg) once a day for 4 days.\n• Brucellosis (with doxycycline): 600 to 900 mg once a day; leprosy: monthly or daily dosing within multidrug therapy.\n• Take on an empty stomach (1 hour before or 2 hours after food)."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• No adjustment is usually needed for renal impairment.\n• Hepatic impairment: use with caution and monitor closely, and avoid in severe liver disease where possible.\n• Dose adjustment may be needed with other hepatotoxic drugs and in older adults."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against Mycobacterium tuberculosis, M. leprae, Staphylococcus aureus (including biofilm), Neisseria meningitidis, Haemophilus influenzae, and Brucella.\n• Common uses: tuberculosis, adjunct therapy for staphylococcal prosthetic material and endocarditis, brucellosis, leprosy, and prophylaxis for meningococcal and Hib contacts.\n• Never use as monotherapy for treatment: resistance emerges within days to weeks."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Used in pregnancy for tuberculosis when needed; give vitamin K to the newborn if the mother used it near term, because of the risk of haemorrhagic disease.\n• Compatible with breastfeeding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Strong inducer of CYP3A4 and other enzymes: lowers levels of warfarin (monitor INR closely), DOACs, oral contraceptives (use non-hormonal contraception), many antiretrovirals including protease inhibitors, tacrolimus, ciclosporin, azole antifungals, methadone, opioids, antiepileptics, corticosteroids, and statins.\n• Review every medicine before starting; stopping rifampicin lets levels of affected drugs rise again within weeks.\n• Hepatotoxicity: check liver function at baseline and regularly, and warn patients about jaundice and dark urine.\n• Orange-red discolouration of urine, tears and sweat (it can stain soft contact lenses).\n• Thrombocytopenia and haemolysis (rare, more frequent with intermittent dosing), flu-like syndrome, and rash."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "polymyxins",
      "title": "Colistin and polymyxin B",
      "category": "Polymyxins",
      "subtitle": "Polymyxins | IV, inhaled, intrathecal",
      "summary": "Last-resort agents for carbapenem-resistant Gram-negatives when newer drugs are unavailable. Nephrotoxic and neurotoxic, and have confusing unit conventions. Current IDSA guidance prefers newer beta-lactam combinations.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Adult dose (consensus)",
          "text": "• Colistin is given as the prodrug colistimethate sodium (CMS), with doses written in millions of international units (MIU) or mg of colistin base activity (CBA), so check the label of your product: 1 MIU is about 80 mg of CMS, which is about 33 mg of CBA.\n• Colistin loading dose: 9 MIU (about 300 mg CBA) IV, regardless of renal function, over 30 minutes.\n• Colistin maintenance at normal renal function: 4.5 MIU every 12 hours (about 9 MIU a day), starting 12 to 24 hours after the loading dose, and reduced by renal function using a nomogram.\n• Polymyxin B: loading dose 2 to 2.5 mg/kg (actual body weight) over 1 hour, then 1.25 to 1.5 mg/kg every 12 hours, usually without renal adjustment.\n• Use combination therapy with a second active drug where possible, and never as monotherapy for serious infection.\n• Inhaled colistin or polymyxin B is used as adjunct therapy for ventilator-associated pneumonia, and intrathecal or intraventricular dosing for CNS infection should be directed by specialists."
        },
        {
          "title": "Renal adjustment",
          "text": "• Colistin: reduce the daily maintenance dose in renal impairment according to creatinine clearance (for example, a lower daily dose when CrCl falls below about 50 mL/min), using a validated nomogram.\n• Haemodialysis: give a supplementary dose on dialysis days according to protocol; continuous renal replacement needs higher doses than intermittent dialysis.\n• Polymyxin B is cleared mainly non-renally, so the dose is usually not adjusted for kidney function."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spectrum and uses",
          "text": "• Active against carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Acinetobacter baumannii.\n• Intrinsically resistant: Proteus, Providencia, Morganella, Serratia, Burkholderia, and all Gram-positive organisms and anaerobes.\n• IDSA AMR guidance prefers newer agents such as ceftazidime-avibactam, meropenem-vaborbactam, imipenem-relebactam, cefiderocol, and sulbactam-durlobactam, and reserves polymyxins for when these are unavailable.\n• Colistin (as CMS, converted to active colistin in urine) is the polymyxin used for urinary infection; polymyxin B should not be used for UTI because urinary levels are poor."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy and lactation",
          "text": "• Avoid unless there are no alternatives; limited data.\n• Limited data, and oral absorption by the infant is poor."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cautions and monitoring",
          "text": "• Nephrotoxicity is common (acute kidney injury in roughly 30 to 50% of patients) and usually reversible: monitor creatinine daily to every other day and avoid other nephrotoxins such as vancomycin, aminoglycosides and contrast.\n• Neurotoxicity: paraesthesia, dizziness, confusion, and neuromuscular blockade with respiratory failure.\n• Inhaled therapy can cause bronchospasm: give a bronchodilator first.\n• Heteroresistance and plasmid-mediated resistance (mcr genes) are increasing, so test susceptibility by broth microdilution.\n• Skin hyperpigmentation with polymyxin B, and pain at the IM injection site (IM use is avoided)."
        }
      ],
      "source": "Compiled from standard references (Sanford Guide, product labelling, BNF). Verify against the current edition. Unit conversions and dosing: 2019 international consensus guidelines for polymyxins. Use positioning: IDSA AMR guidance."
    },
